WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.380
So, have you ever felt like you've hit a wall, you know, conceptually?

00:04.780 --> 00:08.660
Or maybe a system you're dealing with just isn't telling you the full story.

00:08.900 --> 00:10.920
Yeah, that feeling of something missing.

00:11.380 --> 00:11.820
Exactly.

00:12.200 --> 00:17.120
And, well, what if that feeling isn't actually a bug, but it's more like a fundamental feature?

00:17.240 --> 00:23.680
Like, what if incompleteness is actually, by design, an architectural choice, not, you know, a flaw?

00:23.880 --> 00:25.180
That's a fascinating way to put it.

00:25.180 --> 00:37.580
And today, we're really embarking on a deep dive into an idea that kind of unifies everything from really abstract, formal logic, computer architecture, all the way to the, well, the fabric of the cosmos itself.

00:37.780 --> 00:38.020
Wow.

00:38.240 --> 00:39.380
Yeah, the principle of incompleteness.

00:39.420 --> 00:39.620
Yeah.

00:39.800 --> 00:47.340
We'll explore how systems, and this could be mathematical systems, computational ones, even the universe, how they function, precisely because they admit their limits.

00:47.340 --> 00:55.800
They manage their boundaries, and often they explicitly signal their own moments of not being ready, these null states.

00:55.960 --> 00:58.280
Okay, so we've gathered a stack of your sources for this.

00:58.340 --> 01:03.640
We've got deep dives into Karl Fant's really groundbreaking null convention logic.

01:03.800 --> 01:04.540
NCL, yeah.

01:04.540 --> 01:04.780
Yeah.

01:04.960 --> 01:05.800
Foundational stuff.

01:05.980 --> 01:06.220
Right.

01:06.580 --> 01:13.680
And philosophical texts on emergence, some pretty cutting-edge cosmological research on the cosmic microwave background dipole.

01:13.780 --> 01:15.020
The CMB dipole, yep.

01:15.260 --> 01:16.340
Crucial constraints there.

01:16.340 --> 01:20.660
And this big framework, the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum, or RSVP.

01:20.760 --> 01:21.220
That's the one.

01:21.400 --> 01:22.380
A very expansive model.

01:22.680 --> 01:27.480
So our mission here is to really pull out the most important nuggets from all these different fields.

01:27.480 --> 01:39.840
We want to show you how these null signals, this idea of managed incompleteness, acts like a kind of hidden blueprint, something ensuring coherence across huge scales and maybe even reshaping how we understand the universe.

01:40.320 --> 01:40.760
Absolutely.

01:41.060 --> 01:43.540
And this isn't just, you know, abstract theory for academics.

01:43.540 --> 01:49.360
Understanding how systems handle their own incompleteness, how they signal, wait, not ready yet.

01:49.360 --> 01:56.080
It offers really profound insights, insights into how information actually flows, how order emerges from what looks like chaos.

01:56.080 --> 02:07.080
And maybe even why certain technologies or scientific paradigms take hold and spread while others may be just as valid, sometimes even better, get suppressed or just forgotten.

02:07.340 --> 02:12.300
It really gives you a new lens, a new way to look at complexity, at design.

02:12.300 --> 02:17.200
From the tiniest circuit right up to the biggest structures in the cosmos.

02:18.120 --> 02:21.480
We want you to walk away seeing these null signals everywhere.

02:22.260 --> 02:22.380
Oh.

02:22.740 --> 02:23.040
Okay.

02:23.140 --> 02:24.220
So let's untack this.

02:24.500 --> 02:29.780
Maybe starting with one of the most foundational ideas, something that really shook up mathematics back in the early 20th century.

02:30.360 --> 02:32.420
Kurt Gödel's incompleteness theorems.

02:32.500 --> 02:32.640
Right.

02:32.720 --> 02:33.000
Gödel.

02:33.320 --> 02:34.240
Always looms large.

02:34.240 --> 02:34.620
It does.

02:35.040 --> 02:43.720
Essentially, what Gödel showed is that any formal system, think of it like a set of rules and axioms, if it's powerful enough to describe basic arithmetic, you know, adding and multiplying numbers.

02:43.880 --> 02:44.000
Yeah.

02:44.140 --> 02:51.720
It will always contain statements that are true, demonstrably true, but they cannot be proven within that system's own rules using only its own axioms.

02:51.820 --> 02:54.260
So wait, true but unprovable from the inside.

02:54.480 --> 02:54.900
Exactly.

02:55.040 --> 02:56.680
It's a truly profound thing.

02:56.680 --> 03:05.400
The system itself, even if it's perfectly logical, perfectly consistent, it can't fully describe its own truths using just its own toolkit.

03:05.800 --> 03:08.420
It always, in a sense, points beyond itself.

03:08.700 --> 03:12.640
So if I'm getting this right, it's not a flaw then, not a bug we need to squash.

03:12.780 --> 03:15.600
It's more like just how these systems are.

03:15.680 --> 03:15.980
Yeah.

03:16.200 --> 03:16.700
Baked in.

03:17.000 --> 03:21.940
Like a perfectly made box that just can't contain the blueprints for how it was built.

03:22.160 --> 03:24.740
Or maybe even a full map of its own insides.

03:24.740 --> 03:28.860
What does that really mean for a system being blind to some of its own truths?

03:29.300 --> 03:30.460
You've nailed the core point.

03:30.820 --> 03:31.340
Precisely.

03:31.860 --> 03:33.040
Gödel didn't find a bug.

03:33.420 --> 03:36.980
He basically established a baseline reality for these kinds of systems.

03:37.340 --> 03:38.340
They are locally coherent.

03:38.520 --> 03:40.400
They work perfectly fine within their defined rules.

03:40.540 --> 03:43.020
But they are fundamentally globally incomplete.

03:43.260 --> 03:43.440
Okay.

03:43.620 --> 03:48.940
The very act of defining a domain, drawing that boundary around the system, it inherently excludes something.

03:48.940 --> 03:55.380
It excludes the meta-level conditions, the context that actually creates it, or the truths that sit just outside its defined axioms.

03:55.740 --> 04:06.980
So while arithmetic itself can't prove certain true statements about arithmetic, from a metallurgical standpoint, an observer, someone standing outside the system, can often see that they are true.

04:06.980 --> 04:15.880
It's that critical shift in perspective, that meta-level view, that asymmetry, which reveals the real architectural nature of this incompleteness.

04:16.120 --> 04:18.780
And this isn't just floating around in abstract math land, right?

04:19.060 --> 04:25.660
Your sources draw a really direct, compelling line from Gödel straight to Alan Turing's work in computation.

04:25.660 --> 04:30.280
It feels like this idea ripples outwards into the machines we actually build.

04:30.420 --> 04:31.060
That's absolutely right.

04:31.520 --> 04:38.580
Turing's halting problem, which, you know, grew directly out of Gödel's insights, demonstrates this brilliantly in the realm of computation.

04:38.900 --> 04:39.800
The halting problem.

04:39.980 --> 04:40.660
Okay, remind us.

04:40.800 --> 04:53.780
It states that no universal computing machine, think of an idealized computer that can run any program, can definitively determine, for all other arbitrary programs, whether that program will eventually halt, you know, finish its calculation, or just run forever.

04:53.780 --> 04:56.100
Ah, right, the infinite loop problem.

04:56.480 --> 04:57.160
Kind of, yeah.

04:57.500 --> 05:04.800
And just like with Gödel, while a machine itself can't solve this problem generally from within its own operating logic, then how could it?

05:05.060 --> 05:09.720
It might have to simulate the other machine, and if that one never halts, the simulation never halts either.

05:09.800 --> 05:10.780
Right, it gets stuck.

05:11.020 --> 05:16.460
But from a metacomplicational perspective, looking from the outside, we can prove this undecidability.

05:17.000 --> 05:21.360
Both Gödel and Turing, they really highlight this fundamental meta-level asymmetry.

05:21.360 --> 05:29.040
Systems are often blind to the very machinery that constructs them, or relates them, or even predicts their ultimate outcomes.

05:29.460 --> 05:32.420
They operate effectively within their defined local boundaries, sure.

05:32.740 --> 05:38.320
But those boundaries themselves, or what lies beyond them, are inherently invisible to the system itself.

05:38.740 --> 05:40.280
And again, this isn't a limitation to fix.

05:40.340 --> 05:41.340
It's a structural property.

05:41.540 --> 05:43.340
It defines their operational domain.

05:43.340 --> 05:48.040
Okay, here's where it gets really interesting for me, and maybe a bit more down-to-earth.

05:48.720 --> 05:57.920
Your sources connect these really deep mathematical truths, this meta-level blindness, frame-shifting to surprisingly common, even funny examples from daily life.

05:58.180 --> 06:04.220
It kind of drives home how fundamental this idea is, operating within a system versus stepping outside its rules.

06:04.360 --> 06:04.700
Oh, yeah.

06:04.800 --> 06:05.700
The analogies are great.

06:05.820 --> 06:07.200
Think about that classic joke, right?

06:07.200 --> 06:15.020
You draw 1 plus 1 on a piece of paper, then instead of writing 2, you draw the equal sign over the whole thing, turning it into a picture of a window.

06:15.280 --> 06:15.600
Juckles.

06:16.240 --> 06:16.900
I didn't know that one.

06:17.200 --> 06:22.100
It's funny, and it's funny because it completely messes with our expectation of the system's frame.

06:22.360 --> 06:26.180
We automatically assume 1 plus 1 are operating under the rules of arithmetic.

06:26.720 --> 06:29.860
But the physical act of drawing, it literally changes the frame.

06:30.180 --> 06:33.580
It turns math symbols into parts of a visual gag, a window.

06:33.580 --> 06:39.940
The humor, that aha moment, comes from that playful break, that fourth wall break in our mental model.

06:40.080 --> 06:42.420
We're forced outside the assumed math system.

06:42.660 --> 06:50.720
Similarly, you know, if an algebra student is told to find X on a test, and they just circle the letter X in the equation and write, it's right here.

06:50.880 --> 06:51.100
Whoops.

06:51.500 --> 06:51.680
Yeah.

06:51.920 --> 06:54.040
They've done the exact same thing, a meta-level shift.

06:54.040 --> 07:01.780
They moved from treating X as an abstract mathematical variable, needing solving, to treating it as a literal graphical object on the page.

07:02.180 --> 07:10.040
This jump, higher or lower in abstraction, or this kind of fourth wall break revealing the game's implicit rules, that's where humor often kicks in.

07:10.320 --> 07:15.400
It's about playfully flipping the expected system boundaries, exposing those implicit definitions.

07:15.680 --> 07:20.880
These jokes work because we instinctively get that shift, that unexpected leap outside the assumed rules.

07:21.120 --> 07:23.220
Our minds are constantly doing this, managing frames.

07:23.220 --> 07:26.520
So incompleteness isn't just some abstract thing in a math textbook.

07:26.860 --> 07:32.240
It's a structural trick, something our minds, and really all complex systems, do all the time.

07:32.440 --> 07:39.220
We're constantly managing context and frame, and when that frame gets revealed or subverted or even explicitly signaled,

07:39.560 --> 07:43.080
well, it's not just surprising, maybe funny, but it's also how we navigate the world.

07:43.500 --> 07:48.620
It highlights that the system, from its own viewpoint, isn't always giving you the full picture.

07:48.620 --> 07:54.920
Okay, so let's bring this idea, this meta-level blindness and inherent incompleteness, squarely into computer science.

07:54.960 --> 07:55.080
Yeah.

07:55.560 --> 07:56.260
Digital circuits.

07:56.560 --> 08:00.340
Because there, we hit a core challenge that designers have wrestled with for ages.

08:00.820 --> 08:03.000
Standard sequential Boolean logic circuits.

08:03.120 --> 08:04.700
Right, the foundation of most chips today.

08:05.060 --> 08:05.400
Exactly.

08:05.820 --> 08:08.620
And they rely entirely on external timing.

08:08.620 --> 08:16.620
These precisely timed, globally distributed clock signals that have to arrive everywhere at just the right moment to coordinate operations.

08:16.920 --> 08:23.840
These clocks, in a really fundamental way, hide the inherent incompleteness and the potential non-determinism of the system.

08:24.400 --> 08:30.120
Without them, if things just happened whenever they were ready, well, concurrency could become totally chaotic.

08:30.760 --> 08:35.840
Individual logic gates, they don't inherently know when their inputs are fully ready or stable.

08:35.840 --> 08:37.520
Okay, let's unpack that a bit more.

08:37.600 --> 08:44.940
So if you've got lots of things happening at once concurrently, it can turn into a real mess without something like a clock keeping them all in step.

08:45.240 --> 08:54.240
It sounds like the clock is like this external conductor, constantly orchestrating, making sure every part plays at the exact right microsecond, because otherwise they can't hear each other properly.

08:54.700 --> 08:57.220
What happens if that conductor messes up, even slightly?

08:57.580 --> 08:58.540
That's a perfect analogy.

08:58.980 --> 09:03.700
Without that perfect synchronization, you get what engineers call race conditions and hazards.

09:03.700 --> 09:09.880
The final result depends on tiny, unpredictable differences in how fast signals travel down different wires.

09:10.260 --> 09:14.300
It's incredibly hard to design reliably around that in complex systems.

09:14.720 --> 09:19.980
And that's exactly what Carl Vance's null convention logic, or NCL, was designed to address.

09:20.120 --> 09:21.100
NCL, right.

09:21.440 --> 09:24.260
It's a revolutionary way to design asynchronous circuits.

09:24.520 --> 09:26.600
Circuits that don't need a global clock.

09:27.080 --> 09:30.980
They work without that external conductor, eliminating all those tricky timing dependencies.

09:30.980 --> 09:41.720
Phant's key insight, as he put it, was that the coordinating behavior embodied in the mathematician is lost, when you just translate abstract functions into physical circuits that then run on their own.

09:42.460 --> 09:49.200
NCL tries to bring that mathematician's coordination back, embedding the awareness of completeness directly into the logic itself.

09:49.200 --> 09:54.220
It takes the human engineer, or the global clock, out of that coordinating role.

09:54.380 --> 09:55.380
So how does it actually do that?

09:55.440 --> 09:58.200
How do you make a simple logic gate aware of completeness?

09:59.020 --> 10:01.440
Build that sort of meta-level view right into the hardware?

10:01.920 --> 10:04.160
Well, NCL gates are fundamentally different.

10:04.560 --> 10:06.140
They aren't just simple and or gates.

10:06.460 --> 10:09.400
They operate on a principle of explicit request and acknowledgement.

10:09.400 --> 10:19.160
An NCL gate will only assert a valid data result, say a true or a false value, if and only if all of its inputs are valid data.

10:19.380 --> 10:19.600
Okay.

10:19.920 --> 10:21.000
All inputs must be ready.

10:21.440 --> 10:29.800
Critically, if any input is in a special state called NL, meaning not ready or invalid, the output of the gate is also forced to be NL.

10:30.180 --> 10:32.340
This immediately imposes a kind of self-discipline.

10:32.960 --> 10:36.240
No gate can ever produce data if its inputs are incomplete or uncertain.

10:36.240 --> 10:41.000
And this principle is absolutely crucial for achieving what's called delay insensitivity.

10:41.220 --> 10:44.820
Delay insensitivity, meaning it doesn't matter how long signals take to get there.

10:44.940 --> 10:45.340
Exactly.

10:45.720 --> 10:54.420
The circuit works correctly regardless of variations in how long signals take to propagate through wires or gates because the logic itself determines readiness, not some external fragile timer.

10:54.860 --> 11:02.260
It's about using explicit symbolic states, data, and NLL to restore confidence and predictability without any hidden timing assumptions.

11:02.260 --> 11:09.800
So it's almost like a conversation where you literally wait for everyone to finish speaking and confirm they're done before you even start thinking about your reply.

11:10.000 --> 11:14.620
You don't just jump in if someone's still mid-thought or hasn't given their complete valid input.

11:14.840 --> 11:16.240
That's a really good analogy, actually.

11:16.620 --> 11:18.880
NCL circuits operate by constantly cycling.

11:18.880 --> 11:26.520
They move between an all-NL state, which explicitly signals the whole system is reset and ready for new data,

11:26.700 --> 11:30.820
and a data resolution state, where valid data propagates through the circuit.

11:31.240 --> 11:36.600
This NL data cycle ensures inputs are always complete and stable before processing happens.

11:37.020 --> 11:42.180
The circuit intrinsically indicates its own readiness and completion purely, symbolically, autonomously.

11:42.500 --> 11:44.120
No external control needed.

11:44.460 --> 11:46.160
It's self-aware, self-coordinating.

11:46.160 --> 11:47.620
Okay, that cycle makes sense.

11:47.880 --> 11:49.160
But how does it handle the transitions?

11:49.340 --> 11:50.760
You mentioned NLL and data.

11:50.880 --> 11:51.740
Are there other states?

11:51.980 --> 11:52.200
Right.

11:52.540 --> 11:55.740
To achieve truly robust completeness and true delay in sensitivity,

11:56.160 --> 12:00.520
FANT's original most rigorous NCL introduces a four-value logic.

12:01.200 --> 12:04.700
So beyond the standard true and false, it adds intermediate and NLL.

12:04.980 --> 12:05.380
Intermediate.

12:05.440 --> 12:05.680
Yeah.

12:06.020 --> 12:12.660
So a gate, for example, will only output a data result, true or false, if all its inputs are valid data.

12:12.660 --> 12:17.100
Conversely, it will only output an NLL result if all its inputs are NL.

12:17.620 --> 12:19.480
So what does intermediate do?

12:19.580 --> 12:21.340
Is it like a signal of partial readiness?

12:21.480 --> 12:24.480
Like, hang on, I'm not fully NLL anymore, but I'm not quite data either.

12:24.560 --> 12:25.080
I'm transitioning.

12:25.580 --> 12:26.300
Exactly that.

12:26.640 --> 12:28.180
The intermediate value is critical.

12:28.700 --> 12:31.120
It signifies the system is in a transitional phase.

12:31.120 --> 12:35.440
It ensures that the completeness criteria are enforced for both transitions,

12:35.860 --> 12:39.040
going from NL to data, and going from data back to NL.

12:39.520 --> 12:43.840
This allows the circuit to autonomously, unambiguously signal when it's fully reset

12:43.840 --> 12:45.560
and ready for the next wave of data.

12:46.040 --> 12:48.440
For instance, when all the outputs in a section become NLL,

12:48.720 --> 12:52.900
it means without any doubt that that part of the circuit is completely reset and waiting.

12:53.380 --> 12:57.580
This four-value approach is purely delay-insensitive, symbolically complete.

12:57.580 --> 13:01.340
It's the theoretically watertight solution for absolute reliability,

13:01.720 --> 13:03.600
even if signal timings are all over the place.

13:03.860 --> 13:05.320
Four-value sounds complex, though.

13:05.440 --> 13:06.160
Is that practical?

13:06.620 --> 13:08.060
It can be complex to implement.

13:08.800 --> 13:11.920
So while four-value logic offers that theoretical perfection,

13:12.660 --> 13:17.720
a simpler but still extremely effective approach for practical use is the feedback solution.

13:18.400 --> 13:22.260
In this version, each NCL gate acts like a tiny state machine,

13:22.660 --> 13:24.960
and it incorporates a property called hysteresis.

13:25.500 --> 13:25.980
Hysteresis.

13:25.980 --> 13:28.400
Right. That's where the system's output depends,

13:28.520 --> 13:30.680
not just on the current input, but also on its history.

13:30.920 --> 13:33.120
It has a kind of memory of its last state.

13:33.320 --> 13:34.600
Precisely. Well remembered.

13:35.120 --> 13:39.680
This means the gate doesn't instantly flip its output the moment an input changes slightly.

13:40.080 --> 13:43.280
It needs its inputs to cross a certain threshold and stay there,

13:43.400 --> 13:46.240
be stable for a short period, before it changes state.

13:46.560 --> 13:50.840
This prevents rapid, unstable back-and-forth toggling, sometimes called shattering.

13:51.440 --> 13:55.320
Now, technically, this isn't purely delay-insensitive in the strictest theoretical sense.

13:55.320 --> 14:00.060
There's a non-critical timing relationship, where the internal feedback path needs to stabilize

14:00.060 --> 14:01.820
faster than new data waves arrive.

14:02.120 --> 14:07.520
But it's considered effectively delay-insensitive, meaning in practice, it's highly robust,

14:07.940 --> 14:09.800
very reliable for commercial applications.

14:10.180 --> 14:14.360
It strikes a great balance between theoretical purity and engineering reality.

14:14.360 --> 14:19.860
Okay, so four-value for ultimate robustness, three-value feedback for practical robustness.

14:20.200 --> 14:24.660
Can you simplify it even more for, say, integrating with standard digital tech?

14:24.820 --> 14:27.180
Absolutely. And this is key for commercial viability.

14:27.560 --> 14:31.780
NCL can operate using just two conventional voltage levels, like zero volts and five volts,

14:31.860 --> 14:32.940
just like standard logic.

14:33.940 --> 14:35.800
The trick is how you interpret those voltages.

14:36.240 --> 14:40.200
One voltage level is assigned to represent NLL, and the other represents data.

14:40.200 --> 14:44.320
But wait, if you only have data in NLL, how do you represent true and false?

14:44.520 --> 14:48.320
Ah, good question. You use a clever encoding called dual-rail encoding.

14:48.700 --> 14:52.740
Instead of one wire carrying a zero or one for a bit, you use two wires per bit.

14:52.820 --> 14:54.420
Let's call them wire A and wire B.

14:54.800 --> 14:59.340
If wire A has the data voltage and wire B has the NLL voltage, that could represent, say,

14:59.560 --> 15:03.720
logical true. If wire B is data and wire A is NLL, that represents false.

15:03.880 --> 15:05.200
And if both are NLL?

15:05.200 --> 15:10.580
That's the explicit NLL state for that bit. The crucial rule, the convention, is that only one

15:10.580 --> 15:16.520
wire in the pair asserts data at any time. So NCL gates in this setup become discrete threshold

15:16.520 --> 15:21.320
gates. They make decisions based on the number of data inputs they see, not specific voltage

15:21.320 --> 15:27.260
combinations on single wires. This forces the mutually exclusive assertion of data, meaning true

15:27.260 --> 15:32.560
or false, to be explicit, inherent in the logic. It embeds that completeness check right at the

15:32.560 --> 15:33.460
physical signal level.

15:33.980 --> 15:37.980
Wow. What's really hitting me here is that NCL fundamentally changes how we even think

15:37.980 --> 15:42.240
about computation. It's almost like you're supposed to say, it embeds the mathematician

15:42.240 --> 15:47.720
back into the logic. The intelligence of sequencing, coordination, checking for readiness, it's not

15:47.720 --> 15:52.160
external anymore. It's part of the system itself. This feels bigger than just faster chips. It feels

15:52.160 --> 15:55.700
like a more reliable, inherently sound way to compute.

15:56.100 --> 15:59.480
That's a beautiful way to put it. And yes, you've absolutely captured the essence.

15:59.480 --> 16:05.160
The completeness checks, the sequencing logic, that stuff which was once imposed externally

16:05.160 --> 16:11.220
by a human engineer meticulously planning timing, or by a global clock signal, or by complex

16:11.220 --> 16:16.100
software protocols trying to manage concurrency, it's now directly expressed within the symbols

16:16.100 --> 16:22.640
themselves. Data and NLL. It transforms the potential chaos of concurrent operations into

16:22.640 --> 16:27.720
guaranteed, predictable computability, just by encoding the boundaries and making the system

16:27.720 --> 16:33.220
self-aware of those boundaries. This approach gets rid of whole classes of problems, non-deterministic

16:33.220 --> 16:38.540
behavior, race conditions, timing hazards that constantly play traditional concurrent systems.

16:39.080 --> 16:44.060
It leads to circuits that aren't just more reliable, but also more modular, easier to design,

16:44.160 --> 16:49.140
especially for extreme conditions like space or high radiation environments, where precise timing

16:49.140 --> 16:50.180
is just impossible.

16:50.400 --> 16:55.180
And it's funny, we can even see these NCL-like principles, this idea of explicit states signaling

16:55.180 --> 16:59.420
readiness or completion pop up in everyday life, really intuitively. Think about getting your

16:59.420 --> 17:03.960
driver's license. Having no license or maybe failing a test, that's like the NELLO state, right?

17:04.260 --> 17:09.840
No authority to operate the vehicle. Then an intermediate state is having a learner's permit.

17:10.300 --> 17:15.900
It's this special meta state. It acknowledges partial readiness, but ordinary rules don't quite apply yet.

17:16.240 --> 17:19.820
You can only drive under specific conditions like having a licensed adult with you.

17:19.820 --> 17:25.940
And then data is the full license. It certifies that all the inputs, your knowledge test, the practical

17:25.940 --> 17:31.460
evaluation, being the right age, vision test, whatever, are complete and verified. Society doesn't

17:31.460 --> 17:36.580
need some external clock telling you when you can drive. The symbolic marker of the license itself

17:36.580 --> 17:39.220
enforces the completion of prerequisites.

17:39.420 --> 17:42.600
That's a great analogy. Or think about a simple traffic light sequence.

17:42.600 --> 17:46.940
Green or red are like valid data states. Go or stop.

17:47.040 --> 17:47.320
Red.

17:48.040 --> 17:52.040
But amber, the yellow light, is the explicit intermediate state.

17:52.160 --> 17:52.560
The warning.

17:52.620 --> 17:58.020
Exactly. It makes the logic of waiting explicit. It signals the system is neither fully off nor fully

17:58.020 --> 18:03.660
on, but in this transitional preparatory state, it prevents dangerous races at intersections

18:03.660 --> 18:10.000
by ensuring deterministic, predictable transitions. Just like the NELLO signal in NCL or the intermediate

18:10.000 --> 18:15.200
state, amber makes the logic of waiting, the boundary of validity, explicit and universally

18:15.200 --> 18:15.640
understood.

18:15.640 --> 18:20.980
It's fascinating how these patterns repeat. Your sources even connect this idea of a null

18:20.980 --> 18:26.060
state, signifying incompleteness to scripture, specifically Matthew 26.29.

18:26.280 --> 18:27.760
Ah, yes. The withholding of the wine.

18:27.980 --> 18:28.220
Right.

18:28.760 --> 18:33.140
I will not drink again of this fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new with you

18:33.140 --> 18:38.280
in my father's kingdom. That withholding, it's presented as a profound NELL state.

18:38.280 --> 18:43.960
It signals not yet valid to act, a state of incompleteness. The future drinking, then,

18:44.060 --> 18:48.400
is the transition to a valid state, signaling that the necessary conditions, the conditions

18:48.400 --> 18:53.500
of the kingdom, are finally complete. The wine in this interpretation isn't the data itself,

18:53.680 --> 18:58.780
but it acts as the meta symbol, the marker that authorizes the data flow, marking that boundary

18:58.780 --> 19:03.580
between not yet and fulfilled. It's just amazing how this fundamental concept signaling

19:03.580 --> 19:09.360
incompleteness shows up across such wildly different domains, circuits, licenses, scripture.

19:09.580 --> 19:14.320
And this core idea, internalizing incompleteness, embedding that meta-awareness right into the

19:14.320 --> 19:19.920
system. It connects directly to an incredibly profound principle from thermodynamics, Ilya Prigogine's

19:19.920 --> 19:21.500
concept of dissipative structures.

19:21.860 --> 19:23.800
Prigogine. Right. Nobel Prize winner.

19:24.540 --> 19:29.440
Discipative structures. Okay, so what does this mean for us practically? How do these complex

19:29.440 --> 19:36.060
ordered things like a living cell or a hurricane or even a whole galaxy manage to exist and keep

19:36.060 --> 19:41.300
their structure together in a universe that overall is always tending towards more disorder,

19:41.860 --> 19:42.500
towards entropy?

19:42.640 --> 19:47.100
That's the million-dollar question Prigogine tackled. He showed that these ordered systems

19:47.100 --> 19:52.400
think of a swirling whirlpool in a river, intricate living cells, even blazing stars. They don't

19:52.400 --> 19:57.640
magically reduce entropy overall. That would violate the second law of thermodynamics. Instead,

19:57.640 --> 20:02.600
they maintain their internal coherence, their local order, by exporting entropy, disorder,

20:02.800 --> 20:06.860
waste, heat, whatever, at an accelerated rate into their surrounding environment.

20:06.980 --> 20:12.140
Okay, so they clean their own house by dumping the mess outside. In a way, yes, they speed up

20:12.140 --> 20:18.120
entropy production around them to sustain their own highly organized internal state. So a hurricane,

20:18.260 --> 20:23.400
for example. It maintains that incredible swirling organized structure, not because it's a low

20:23.400 --> 20:28.420
entropy system itself, but because it's a dissipative system. It rapidly dissipates enormous amounts of

20:28.420 --> 20:33.980
heat and energy into the surrounding atmosphere. It creates local order, but at the cost of increasing

20:33.980 --> 20:36.740
the environment's disorder much faster than would happen otherwise.

20:36.740 --> 20:42.140
So order isn't free. It always comes at a cost, like a hidden thermodynamic debt that's constantly

20:42.140 --> 20:47.160
being pushed outwards away from the system itself. It really does sound like the universe's way of

20:47.160 --> 20:49.500
sweeping dirt under the rug, thermodynamically speaking.

20:49.500 --> 20:54.280
Exactly. And notice, this is the same fundamental pattern we saw with Gödel's meta-level,

20:54.520 --> 20:59.800
needing to exist outside the system, and FANCE-NCL, internalizing the coordination that clocks or

20:59.800 --> 21:05.820
engineers used to do externally. These dissipative systems, they appear self-contained, they look

21:05.820 --> 21:12.320
autonomous, but their very existence, their perceived autonomy, is sustained by this hidden superset,

21:12.680 --> 21:17.060
the embedding environment, that continuously absorbs their exported entropy, their uncertainty,

21:17.060 --> 21:22.200
their cost. The whirlpool looks stable and self-contained, but it only exists because the

21:22.200 --> 21:26.900
larger river carries away its turbulence and disorder. It's a local illusion of completeness

21:26.900 --> 21:30.100
maintained by globally incomplete externalized management.

21:30.100 --> 21:32.660
And you actually call this the law of superset entropy.

21:33.220 --> 21:39.200
Yes, precisely. It's a way to capture this general principle. Any ordered system maintains itself by

21:39.200 --> 21:44.680
accelerating entropy production in its environment. The system's apparent autonomy is sustained by a hidden

21:44.680 --> 21:50.680
superset that continuously absorbs its exported entropy. This law reveals that incompleteness

21:50.680 --> 21:56.940
isn't just conceptual, it's structural, it's thermodynamic. A system often cannot fully contain

21:56.940 --> 22:02.580
the conditions or the costs of its own maintenance, so it necessarily relies on externalizing some aspect

22:02.580 --> 22:03.380
of its existence.

22:03.820 --> 22:09.840
Can we see this pattern in, say, computation or AI? How does this externalization play out there?

22:09.840 --> 22:14.700
Oh, absolutely. We see it clearly, and it highlights how engineering choices often reflect these deeper

22:14.700 --> 22:19.360
principles, sometimes unconsciously. Think again about traditional sequental Boolean logic,

22:19.580 --> 22:24.960
the thing Fant was critiquing. The cost of ensuring correct, hazard-free results, managing the precise

22:24.960 --> 22:29.680
timing, preventing race conditions, handling memory access without conflicts. Where does that cost go?

22:29.800 --> 22:31.820
It's pushed outside the logic gates themselves.

22:32.060 --> 22:34.260
Right, onto the designer or the clock network.

22:34.260 --> 22:39.080
Exactly. It's borne by the human engineer who has to meticulously design the clock cycles,

22:39.600 --> 22:43.840
verify the timing across the whole chip, or by the complex external clock circuits

22:43.840 --> 22:49.400
and sophisticated verification software. The circuit itself is blind to all this coordinating behavior.

22:49.760 --> 22:55.800
It relies completely on this externalized control, this hidden superset of design effort and hardware.

22:56.180 --> 22:58.620
Okay, that makes sense for hardware. What about AI?

22:58.620 --> 23:06.120
A very similar pattern emerges. Consider Monica Anderson's sharp critiques of what's called GECO-FAI,

23:06.400 --> 23:10.060
good old-fashioned AI, the symbolic AI that was dominant for decades.

23:10.800 --> 23:16.140
Anderson points out a parallel externalization, the cost of grounding meaning, of providing an ontology

23:16.140 --> 23:21.280
defining what words actually mean, how concepts relate to each other, building a comprehensive knowledge base

23:21.280 --> 23:23.600
about the world that cost is pushed onto the human programmer.

23:23.860 --> 23:27.100
The AI doesn't learn cat means furry pet. The human tells it.

23:27.100 --> 23:32.040
Precisely. The AI doesn't typically learn its core concepts from wrong experience.

23:32.680 --> 23:36.080
The human supplies them, meticulously crafting the semantic scaffolding,

23:36.360 --> 23:38.560
the knowledge of representation for the AI to work with.

23:38.980 --> 23:43.340
So in both Fant's critique of Boolean logic and Anderson's critique of GECO-FAI,

23:43.860 --> 23:48.720
the apparent efficiency and local coherence of the core system relies heavily on hidden,

23:49.160 --> 23:50.700
externalized labor and resources.

23:51.140 --> 23:53.240
The internal system looks neat and complete,

23:53.240 --> 23:59.480
but the externalized burden of incompleteness, of providing context and meaning, is substantial.

23:59.980 --> 24:03.320
So it feels like systems, biological, computational, social, whatever,

24:03.840 --> 24:08.240
have these two main strategies for dealing with this inherent cost of staying ordered,

24:08.760 --> 24:10.580
managing incompleteness, handling entropy.

24:10.820 --> 24:11.780
That's a great way to frame it.

24:12.040 --> 24:14.440
One strategy is what we can call superset dependence.

24:14.820 --> 24:17.900
You push the entropy, the uncertainty, the cost, the difficult stuff outward.

24:17.900 --> 24:23.140
You rely on hidden scaffolding or an external environment or maybe even other agents to manage the disorder.

24:23.360 --> 24:26.700
This is often the default strategy, the path of least resistance initially.

24:26.960 --> 24:29.980
Think of modern ride-sharing platforms like Uber or Lyft.

24:30.360 --> 24:33.380
To the end user, they appear incredibly efficient, seamless.

24:33.760 --> 24:35.180
Just tap a button, a car appears.

24:35.920 --> 24:38.800
But they externalize huge amounts of cost and complexity.

24:39.600 --> 24:43.400
Labor costs, vehicle maintenance, insurance risks, scheduling complexities,

24:43.400 --> 24:49.620
all pushed onto a hidden superset of independent drivers and, to some extent, the clients managing their own time.

24:50.400 --> 24:56.060
The company's lean, efficient front-end operation relies fundamentally on this massive, externalized base,

24:56.480 --> 24:58.280
managing its own disorder and cost.

24:59.240 --> 25:03.860
Abstractly, in category theory, this functional pattern resembles a Coe-Kleisley process,

25:04.040 --> 25:10.360
where the system explicitly interacts with and pushes burdens onto an external, often messy environment.

25:10.460 --> 25:13.040
Okay, so that's pushing it out. What's the alternative? Bringing it in?

25:13.040 --> 25:16.040
Precisely. The other strategy is superset internalization.

25:16.760 --> 25:21.140
This means you fundamentally reconfigure the system's internal dynamics to handle the complexity,

25:21.700 --> 25:23.940
the entropy management, within its own boundaries.

25:24.040 --> 25:25.040
Like NCL does.

25:25.320 --> 25:26.580
It's exactly like NCL does.

25:27.080 --> 25:31.880
By embedding those completeness checks, those NLL signals, directly within its gates,

25:32.400 --> 25:35.720
NCL makes the circuit itself self-aware of input readiness

25:35.720 --> 25:40.160
and internalizes the coordination task previously handled by the external clock.

25:40.600 --> 25:44.860
In modern AI, you see a similar trend with model-free methods in reinforcement learning.

25:45.180 --> 25:49.720
Instead of humans handcrafting a detailed model of the world or providing an ontology,

25:50.120 --> 25:51.440
externalizing the modeling labor,

25:51.440 --> 25:56.260
these methods learn control policies directly from raw sensory inputs and rewards.

25:56.900 --> 26:01.060
They internalize the modeling labor, learning representations directly from interaction.

26:02.200 --> 26:04.660
Categorically, this is more like a Kleisley process,

26:04.900 --> 26:08.980
where the system itself absorbs and transforms the external effects or complexities.

26:08.980 --> 26:12.340
In a framework like RSVP, which we'll get to,

26:12.820 --> 26:17.120
the fundamental fields themselves, scalar capacity, vector flow, and tropic modulus,

26:17.500 --> 26:21.040
can be seen as carrying these ordered burdens, internalizing the dynamics.

26:21.820 --> 26:24.700
Entropy production accumulates across these interacting fields

26:24.700 --> 26:27.740
unless some distributive absorption mechanism kicks in,

26:28.100 --> 26:32.100
effectively allowing the system to internalize and recompile its externalized work,

26:32.240 --> 26:35.780
much like NCL brings that mathematician's coordination inside the logic.

26:35.780 --> 26:39.180
Okay, this whole idea of what gets pushed out versus brought in,

26:39.240 --> 26:41.060
these hidden costs, suppressed alternatives,

26:41.340 --> 26:43.880
it leads us directly to another really fascinating,

26:44.180 --> 26:47.760
maybe slightly disturbing concept from your sources, encoding ratchets.

26:48.180 --> 26:51.240
It suggests the world we have isn't necessarily the world that had to be.

26:51.460 --> 26:52.740
This is such a critical insight, yeah.

26:53.180 --> 26:56.200
It offers a powerful counter-narrative to that smooth story

26:56.200 --> 26:59.120
of inevitable progress we often tell ourselves.

27:00.020 --> 27:02.320
Histories of media, machines, scientific model,

27:02.400 --> 27:04.900
they often look so linear in hindsight, right?

27:04.900 --> 27:08.680
Like technology or ideas, just marched inevitably towards perfection.

27:09.040 --> 27:11.680
Yeah, like of course we ended up with smartphones and the internet.

27:11.820 --> 27:15.780
Right, but often what we're actually seeing are these encoding ratchets.

27:16.500 --> 27:19.540
Basically arbitrary choices about how to represent things,

27:19.600 --> 27:21.720
how to encode information made early on.

27:22.020 --> 27:26.700
Often for very pragmatic reasons, cost, compatibility with existing stuff,

27:26.780 --> 27:27.660
maybe military needs.

27:28.040 --> 27:29.880
But once these encodings get standardized,

27:30.140 --> 27:31.880
once they start to scale really efficiently,

27:31.880 --> 27:36.960
they then tend to foreclose or suppress alternative ways of doing things,

27:37.360 --> 27:38.300
alternative encodings,

27:38.480 --> 27:41.100
even if those alternatives might have been better in the long run,

27:41.220 --> 27:44.520
or richer, or more aligned with how humans actually think.

27:44.840 --> 27:47.160
It's not about being the best encoding possible,

27:47.160 --> 27:50.060
but the one that scaled best, given the constraints of the time.

27:50.480 --> 27:51.380
Can you give us an example?

27:51.520 --> 27:53.620
How did this play out in something we kind of take for granted,

27:53.760 --> 27:55.040
like how movies developed?

27:55.420 --> 27:56.960
Cinema is a perfect example.

27:56.960 --> 28:03.040
Very early on, it ratcheted into representing movies as sequences of 2D frames,

28:03.560 --> 28:06.760
printed on reels of film, projected onto a flat screen.

28:07.380 --> 28:12.160
This encoding turned out to be exceptionally good for mass duplication printing copies of films,

28:12.320 --> 28:14.380
and for projection in standardized theaters.

28:14.960 --> 28:17.940
It scaled incredibly well, economically and logistically.

28:18.120 --> 28:20.080
Which made cinema a global phenomenon.

28:20.300 --> 28:20.720
Exactly.

28:20.880 --> 28:22.140
It became a global monoculture.

28:22.140 --> 28:26.200
The same film could be shown everywhere, relatively cheaply and efficiently.

28:26.640 --> 28:30.520
That 2D frame on a real encoding became the dominant standard,

28:30.660 --> 28:34.040
shaping the entire industry in our experience of movies for a century.

28:34.220 --> 28:36.640
But were there other ways, other roads not taken,

28:36.760 --> 28:38.880
that could have given us a completely different kind of cinema?

28:39.060 --> 28:39.660
Oh, absolutely.

28:40.140 --> 28:43.440
Think about early experiments with stereoscopic systems, 3D movies.

28:43.820 --> 28:46.460
They hinted at what you might call volumetric cinema,

28:46.940 --> 28:50.660
encoding films not as flat frames, but as truly three-dimensional scenes.

28:50.660 --> 28:54.620
Imagine a future where maybe viewers could have slightly different perspectives

28:54.620 --> 28:57.280
depending on where they sat, a more immersive experience.

28:57.700 --> 28:59.980
Or consider something called local actor substitution.

29:01.020 --> 29:05.400
In the 1930s, some studios actually experimented with reshooting entire films

29:05.400 --> 29:07.580
with local casts for different language markets,

29:07.760 --> 29:09.620
rather than just dubbing or subtitling.

29:10.080 --> 29:13.380
These paths, volumetric cinema or local actor substitution,

29:13.840 --> 29:14.660
they got suppressed.

29:15.160 --> 29:16.840
Not because the ideas were bad,

29:16.920 --> 29:19.300
but because they just couldn't scale cheaply or easily.

29:19.300 --> 29:22.020
With the prevailing technologies for duplication,

29:22.380 --> 29:25.220
distribution, and projection at that critical time.

29:25.720 --> 29:30.120
The simple, mass-producible 2D frame standard won out.

29:30.680 --> 29:32.300
It became the encoding ratchet.

29:32.680 --> 29:35.740
And this sounds like it must be a huge theme in computer science too, right?

29:35.800 --> 29:38.840
With how fast things change and how quickly standards lock in.

29:38.960 --> 29:41.100
It's absolutely central to the history of computing.

29:41.500 --> 29:43.800
Computing largely ratcheted into using ASCII,

29:43.900 --> 29:45.700
a standard set of characters and keyboards,

29:46.080 --> 29:48.120
as its primary way of interacting with data.

29:48.120 --> 29:52.200
This wasn't necessarily because it was the best way for humans to interact with information,

29:52.200 --> 29:55.000
but it was driven heavily by early military needs

29:55.000 --> 29:59.080
and compatibility with existing teletype and telecommunication systems.

29:59.280 --> 30:00.320
So text became king.

30:00.680 --> 30:01.620
Text became king.

30:02.160 --> 30:06.660
And this encoding profoundly shaped computers as primarily linear text processors.

30:07.120 --> 30:11.060
It's a lineage you can trace directly to the architecture of today's large language models,

30:11.260 --> 30:13.840
which fundamentally operate on sequences of text tokens.

30:13.840 --> 30:17.100
Keyboard and screen, text-based interaction became the norm,

30:17.540 --> 30:19.780
dictating how we interfaced with computers for decades,

30:19.920 --> 30:22.840
even though our brains work much more visually and associatively.

30:23.360 --> 30:25.100
And the hardware architecture played a role too.

30:25.400 --> 30:25.900
Crucially.

30:26.540 --> 30:27.760
The von Neumann architecture,

30:28.640 --> 30:31.460
the idea of a central processing unit, CPU,

30:32.040 --> 30:35.520
separate from a main memory bank where both instructions and data are stored,

30:35.720 --> 30:37.280
became the dominant standard.

30:37.280 --> 30:41.320
It was brilliantly simple, and it scaled incredibly well with Moore's law,

30:41.760 --> 30:43.260
the exponential growth of transistors,

30:43.420 --> 30:45.840
but it also created a fundamental choke point,

30:46.020 --> 30:48.340
the infamous von Neumann bottleneck.

30:48.420 --> 30:49.300
The bottleneck.

30:49.520 --> 30:53.060
Because data has to constantly shuttle back and forth between the CPU and memory

30:53.060 --> 30:54.960
over a relatively narrow path.

30:55.360 --> 30:55.820
Precisely.

30:56.500 --> 30:59.720
This physical bottleneck reinforces sequential processing.

31:00.420 --> 31:01.060
Fetch instruction.

31:01.460 --> 31:01.940
Fetch data.

31:02.140 --> 31:02.560
Compute.

31:02.980 --> 31:03.560
Store result.

31:03.740 --> 31:03.960
Repeat.

31:03.960 --> 31:08.060
It further cemented that ACETEX stream paradigm,

31:08.520 --> 31:10.000
making it harder for alternative,

31:10.400 --> 31:12.620
more parallel architectures to compete effectively,

31:13.040 --> 31:15.220
even if they might have matched human cognition better.

31:15.580 --> 31:19.320
So what were some of these roads not taken in computing?

31:19.500 --> 31:22.240
The ideas that could have led us down different paths,

31:22.580 --> 31:24.860
maybe towards computers that felt less like text machines?

31:25.220 --> 31:28.060
Your sources mentioned several really fascinating ones

31:28.060 --> 31:30.160
that were explored but didn't achieve mass adoption.

31:30.160 --> 31:31.740
There were recursive machines,

31:31.740 --> 31:34.640
think early IBM handwriting recognizers from the 50s,

31:34.780 --> 31:35.520
or much later,

31:35.900 --> 31:37.980
Apple's Newton PDA in the 90s.

31:38.300 --> 31:39.440
These explored pen input,

31:39.580 --> 31:40.480
graphical interaction,

31:40.840 --> 31:42.300
moving beyond the keyboard constraint.

31:42.340 --> 31:42.900
Right, the Newton.

31:43.240 --> 31:44.220
Ahead of its time, maybe.

31:44.340 --> 31:44.600
Exactly.

31:44.940 --> 31:46.280
Then there were flashcard machines.

31:46.880 --> 31:49.940
Visionary ideas like Vannevar Bush's Memex from the 1940s,

31:50.040 --> 31:51.820
or B.F. Skinner's teaching machines.

31:51.820 --> 31:54.620
These treated computation more like memory augmentation,

31:54.840 --> 31:55.720
information retrieval,

31:56.040 --> 31:58.940
association using modular, card-based systems,

31:59.340 --> 32:00.480
not just linear streams.

32:00.620 --> 32:01.860
Like a personal knowledge base?

32:02.100 --> 32:02.480
Exactly.

32:02.860 --> 32:04.920
And critically, there were data flow architectures.

32:05.180 --> 32:06.300
These were fundamentally different.

32:06.760 --> 32:08.860
Programs weren't sequential instruction lists,

32:09.380 --> 32:11.580
but graphs showing data dependencies.

32:12.660 --> 32:15.300
Computations would happen whenever their required data inputs

32:15.300 --> 32:16.060
became available,

32:16.440 --> 32:18.360
making them inherently parallel and asynchronous,

32:18.800 --> 32:20.760
much more like NCL, in a way.

32:20.760 --> 32:22.320
So why didn't these take off?

32:22.700 --> 32:24.340
They showed immense conceptual promise,

32:25.040 --> 32:27.060
but they just couldn't scale cheaply or efficiently

32:27.060 --> 32:28.440
with the dominant sequential,

32:28.680 --> 32:30.900
transistor-based hardware manufacturing processes

32:30.900 --> 32:31.660
of their time.

32:32.160 --> 32:33.940
They were often technically sound,

32:34.320 --> 32:35.140
maybe even brilliant,

32:35.480 --> 32:37.300
but economically unviable

32:37.300 --> 32:39.260
at those crucial inflection points.

32:39.580 --> 32:40.680
So they got suppressed,

32:40.880 --> 32:42.620
or relegated to niche applications,

32:42.920 --> 32:44.820
while the von Neumann model rolled on.

32:45.160 --> 32:47.080
Okay, this connects directly to Richard Sutton's

32:47.080 --> 32:48.620
famous bitter lesson in AI.

32:48.620 --> 32:50.380
The idea that, historically,

32:50.560 --> 32:51.940
the biggest gains haven't come from

32:51.940 --> 32:53.940
clever human-designed knowledge systems,

32:54.120 --> 32:56.440
but just from scaling up very general methods

32:56.440 --> 32:58.000
with more compute power and data,

32:58.220 --> 32:59.740
it's often used today to justify

32:59.740 --> 33:01.240
the massive deep learning models.

33:01.460 --> 33:03.140
That's the common interpretation, yes.

33:04.100 --> 33:05.420
And Sutton makes a compelling case

33:05.420 --> 33:06.580
based on recent history.

33:07.380 --> 33:08.400
But a counter-reading,

33:08.760 --> 33:09.880
suggested by your sources,

33:10.480 --> 33:12.420
introduces a crucial element of contingency.

33:12.760 --> 33:14.400
It wasn't necessarily inevitable.

33:14.560 --> 33:14.820
Reading.

33:14.820 --> 33:18.120
Meaning, those earlier symbolic AI systems

33:18.120 --> 33:19.380
that GeofAI approaches,

33:19.900 --> 33:21.800
based on human-defined rules and knowledge,

33:22.280 --> 33:23.280
they might not have failed

33:23.280 --> 33:25.820
because their core ideas were fundamentally wrong.

33:26.020 --> 33:27.780
They might have failed simply because

33:27.780 --> 33:30.060
they lacked the scale of computation we have today.

33:30.720 --> 33:32.580
The massively parallel GPUs,

33:32.660 --> 33:34.080
the distributed cloud infrastructure,

33:34.600 --> 33:35.940
the internet-scale data sets.

33:36.600 --> 33:38.540
GeofAI basically brought a knife,

33:38.700 --> 33:40.620
a very intricate, well-crafted knife,

33:40.800 --> 33:42.140
to a compute gunfight.

33:42.140 --> 33:43.620
It just couldn't win at that time.

33:43.780 --> 33:45.220
So the bitter lesson might be less

33:45.220 --> 33:46.440
about the inherent superiority

33:46.440 --> 33:48.300
of scaled general methods

33:48.300 --> 33:50.340
and more about scaling amplifying

33:50.340 --> 33:52.120
whatever encoding happened to be dominant

33:52.120 --> 33:53.480
when compute became cheap.

33:53.680 --> 33:55.020
That's the counter-argument.

33:55.620 --> 33:57.300
Scaling massively amplifies

33:57.300 --> 33:59.220
what was already ratcheted in

33:59.220 --> 34:01.960
by earlier architectural and encoding choices.

34:02.380 --> 34:03.840
It doesn't necessarily prove

34:03.840 --> 34:06.000
that the scaled approach was the only path,

34:06.160 --> 34:07.600
or even the optimal path,

34:07.680 --> 34:08.680
for achieving intelligence.

34:09.320 --> 34:10.420
If compute had been cheap

34:10.420 --> 34:11.860
in parallel much earlier,

34:12.480 --> 34:14.080
maybe data flow architectures

34:14.080 --> 34:15.760
or sophisticated symbolic AI

34:15.760 --> 34:18.040
would have become the dominant ratchet instead.

34:18.280 --> 34:19.420
History could have been very different.

34:19.980 --> 34:20.120
Wow.

34:20.440 --> 34:21.420
And even cosmology,

34:21.600 --> 34:22.780
the study of the entire universe,

34:22.900 --> 34:24.260
even that isn't immune

34:24.260 --> 34:25.440
to these encoding ratchets.

34:25.620 --> 34:26.640
That's kind of mind-blowing.

34:26.940 --> 34:28.480
Our view of cosmic reality itself

34:28.480 --> 34:30.260
might be shaped by these historical choices.

34:30.740 --> 34:32.060
That's perhaps the most striking

34:32.060 --> 34:34.360
and maybe unsettling example, yes.

34:34.980 --> 34:36.560
The standard model in cosmology,

34:36.800 --> 34:38.560
medium, lambda, cold, dark matter,

34:38.560 --> 34:40.100
that's been incredibly successful.

34:40.220 --> 34:41.800
It's vast amounts of data

34:41.800 --> 34:43.480
from the cosmic microwave background

34:43.480 --> 34:44.860
to the distribution of galaxies.

34:45.180 --> 34:47.020
But it represents a particular encoding

34:47.020 --> 34:48.320
of the universe's structure,

34:48.500 --> 34:49.640
its inhomogeneities.

34:49.840 --> 34:50.320
How so?

34:50.440 --> 34:51.740
What's the encoding choice there?

34:51.960 --> 34:53.100
It fundamentally treats

34:53.100 --> 34:54.460
the lumps and bumps in the universe,

34:55.100 --> 34:56.840
the galaxies, the clusters, the voids,

34:57.080 --> 34:58.400
as small perturbations,

34:58.800 --> 35:00.040
small deviations on top

35:00.040 --> 35:01.080
of a perfectly smooth,

35:01.440 --> 35:03.260
uniformly expanding background space.

35:03.660 --> 35:05.260
The math starts with smooth,

35:05.580 --> 35:06.500
then adds corrections.

35:06.500 --> 35:08.980
So it assumes this idealized,

35:09.240 --> 35:10.960
smooth universe as the baseline,

35:11.320 --> 35:12.840
and then just adds little bumps

35:12.840 --> 35:14.320
and wrinkles to match observations,

35:14.680 --> 35:15.680
rather than starting

35:15.680 --> 35:17.300
with the bumpy, wrinkly reality

35:17.300 --> 35:18.320
of voids and clusters

35:18.320 --> 35:19.440
as the primary thing.

35:19.880 --> 35:21.100
It sounds like choosing

35:21.100 --> 35:22.600
to describe a mountain range

35:22.600 --> 35:24.120
by starting with a flat plane

35:24.120 --> 35:25.780
and adding lots of little adjustments.

35:26.000 --> 35:27.100
That's a great analogy.

35:27.380 --> 35:27.760
Exactly.

35:28.540 --> 35:29.900
This frame-based encoding,

35:30.140 --> 35:30.980
starting with a smooth

35:30.980 --> 35:32.980
Friedman-Lemaître-Robertson-Walker metric,

35:33.480 --> 35:34.460
once it was established

35:34.460 --> 35:36.360
and shown to fit the data reasonably well,

35:36.440 --> 35:37.600
especially the CMB data,

35:38.140 --> 35:38.900
it got reinforced.

35:39.820 --> 35:41.340
Computational simulations were built

35:41.340 --> 35:42.960
assuming these small perturbations,

35:43.320 --> 35:44.360
and then when observations

35:44.360 --> 35:45.900
showed the expansion was accelerating,

35:46.320 --> 35:47.440
this encoding forced us

35:47.440 --> 35:48.380
to invent concepts

35:48.380 --> 35:50.000
like the posmological constant,

35:50.360 --> 35:51.780
lambda, or dark energy,

35:51.980 --> 35:53.400
to explain the acceleration

35:53.400 --> 35:55.240
within that smooth background framework.

35:55.600 --> 35:58.060
But were there alternative encodings,

35:58.180 --> 35:59.320
ways to describe the universe

35:59.320 --> 36:00.860
starting from the structure itself?

36:00.860 --> 36:01.540
Yes.

36:02.180 --> 36:03.580
Alternative approaches exist.

36:03.960 --> 36:05.580
Things like Bouchert averaging schemes

36:05.580 --> 36:07.800
or effective fluid models

36:07.800 --> 36:09.380
like work by Gianni and colleagues

36:09.380 --> 36:10.600
expected around 2025.

36:11.420 --> 36:12.600
These take a different approach.

36:12.880 --> 36:14.120
They treat the inhomogeneities,

36:14.540 --> 36:15.860
the collapsing regions of matter,

36:15.940 --> 36:16.880
and the expanding voids

36:16.880 --> 36:18.620
not as negligible perturbations,

36:18.980 --> 36:19.940
but as primary,

36:20.200 --> 36:21.840
dynamically significant sources

36:21.840 --> 36:22.820
of stress energy

36:22.820 --> 36:25.040
that affect the overall expansion directly.

36:25.480 --> 36:26.660
They offer a richer,

36:26.860 --> 36:28.960
non-linear encoding of cosmic reality,

36:28.960 --> 36:30.340
one where the back reaction

36:30.340 --> 36:31.620
of structure on geometry

36:31.620 --> 36:32.580
is taken seriously.

36:33.320 --> 36:33.720
And interestingly,

36:33.980 --> 36:34.720
this re-encoding,

36:35.060 --> 36:36.040
by properly accounting

36:36.040 --> 36:37.620
for the universe's lumpy texture,

36:38.060 --> 36:39.000
might even help resolve

36:39.000 --> 36:40.000
some major tensions

36:40.000 --> 36:41.140
in modern cosmology,

36:41.460 --> 36:42.140
like the disagreement

36:42.140 --> 36:43.460
over the Hubble expansion rate,

36:43.680 --> 36:45.280
not by inventing new exotic physics,

36:45.540 --> 36:46.460
but simply by admitting

36:46.460 --> 36:47.920
our initial smooth encoding

36:47.920 --> 36:49.020
might have been too simplistic.

36:49.760 --> 36:51.060
RSVP, which we're coming to,

36:51.340 --> 36:52.300
pushes this even further.

36:52.520 --> 36:53.620
So what's the big takeaway

36:53.620 --> 36:54.620
from all this talk

36:54.620 --> 36:55.760
about encoding ratchets,

36:55.800 --> 36:56.400
it feels like?

36:56.400 --> 36:59.180
It means the truths we hold

36:59.180 --> 37:01.000
in science and technology,

37:01.320 --> 37:02.420
they're often not

37:02.420 --> 37:04.220
these absolute inevitable things.

37:04.360 --> 37:05.280
They're shaped by history,

37:05.380 --> 37:05.980
by economics,

37:06.120 --> 37:07.400
by almost accidental choices

37:07.400 --> 37:08.220
made long ago.

37:08.660 --> 37:09.720
What scaled efficiently

37:09.720 --> 37:10.640
might not have been

37:10.640 --> 37:12.080
what was objectively optimal

37:12.080 --> 37:13.020
or the most insightful.

37:13.340 --> 37:14.900
It really encourages us,

37:15.000 --> 37:16.060
maybe even obligates us,

37:16.120 --> 37:17.000
to look for those

37:17.000 --> 37:18.180
suppressed alternatives,

37:18.440 --> 37:19.500
those roads not taken.

37:19.600 --> 37:20.360
They might hold keys

37:20.360 --> 37:21.120
we've overlooked.

37:21.520 --> 37:22.220
Beautifully put.

37:22.540 --> 37:23.800
It's a call for a certain kind

37:23.800 --> 37:24.900
of intellectual humility

37:24.900 --> 37:27.000
and maybe intellectual archaeology.

37:27.000 --> 37:28.200
Okay, this whole idea

37:28.200 --> 37:29.760
of roads not taken,

37:29.980 --> 37:31.180
of needing to re-examine

37:31.180 --> 37:32.080
fundamental assumptions

37:32.080 --> 37:32.980
and maybe look at

37:32.980 --> 37:34.000
suppressed alternatives,

37:34.500 --> 37:35.460
leads us perfectly

37:35.460 --> 37:36.860
into this relativistic

37:36.860 --> 37:38.280
scalar vector plenum

37:38.280 --> 37:39.720
framework RSVP

37:39.720 --> 37:40.300
that your sources

37:40.300 --> 37:41.000
discuss so much.

37:41.060 --> 37:41.820
It really sounds like

37:41.820 --> 37:42.820
a radical rethinking,

37:43.340 --> 37:44.120
deliberately trying

37:44.120 --> 37:44.980
to step outside

37:44.980 --> 37:45.700
those existing

37:45.700 --> 37:46.940
encoding ratchets

37:46.940 --> 37:47.660
in cosmology

37:47.660 --> 37:48.940
and maybe even intelligence.

37:49.600 --> 37:50.020
Indeed.

37:50.480 --> 37:51.320
That's a great way

37:51.320 --> 37:52.240
to see RSVP.

37:52.780 --> 37:53.740
It can definitely be viewed

37:53.740 --> 37:54.860
as a conscious effort

37:54.860 --> 37:55.640
to reopen

37:55.640 --> 37:56.240
some of those

37:56.240 --> 37:57.360
suppressed trajectories,

37:57.780 --> 37:58.700
both in how we

37:58.700 --> 37:59.740
understand intelligence

37:59.740 --> 38:00.740
and how we model

38:00.740 --> 38:01.240
the cosmos.

38:02.220 --> 38:03.040
It does this

38:03.040 --> 38:03.980
by treating phenomena

38:03.980 --> 38:04.900
as fundamental

38:04.900 --> 38:06.240
field theoretic processes

38:06.240 --> 38:07.420
rather than relying

38:07.420 --> 38:08.240
solely on

38:08.240 --> 38:09.420
particle-based descriptions

38:09.420 --> 38:11.180
or purely symbolic logic,

38:11.380 --> 38:11.940
which were parts

38:11.940 --> 38:13.280
of those earlier ratchets.

38:13.580 --> 38:14.840
It posits a universe

38:14.840 --> 38:15.440
that isn't just

38:15.440 --> 38:16.020
empty space

38:16.020 --> 38:16.620
with particles

38:16.620 --> 38:17.280
sprinkled in,

38:17.600 --> 38:18.320
but a dynamic

38:18.320 --> 38:19.540
interacting plenum,

38:19.920 --> 38:20.900
a universal filling

38:20.900 --> 38:21.820
made of fields.

38:21.820 --> 38:23.220
Okay, so what are

38:23.220 --> 38:24.080
the core components

38:24.080 --> 38:24.940
of this plenum?

38:25.120 --> 38:26.380
What are the fundamental

38:26.380 --> 38:27.940
fields in RSVP?

38:28.240 --> 38:28.920
At its heart,

38:29.320 --> 38:30.420
RSVP posits

38:30.420 --> 38:31.520
three fundamental

38:31.520 --> 38:33.020
deeply interlinked fields

38:33.020 --> 38:33.860
that constitute

38:33.860 --> 38:34.540
this plenum,

38:34.640 --> 38:35.600
this fabric of existence.

38:36.060 --> 38:36.680
First, there's

38:36.680 --> 38:38.040
the scalar capacity field.

38:38.380 --> 38:38.980
You can think of this

38:38.980 --> 38:40.160
as RSVP's version

38:40.160 --> 38:41.540
of a vacuum energy field,

38:41.860 --> 38:43.120
a kind of energetic potential

38:43.120 --> 38:44.540
inherent in space itself.

38:44.700 --> 38:45.440
It's what drives

38:45.440 --> 38:46.140
this key concept

38:46.140 --> 38:47.020
called falling outward.

38:47.300 --> 38:47.860
Falling outward.

38:48.160 --> 38:48.600
We'll definitely

38:48.600 --> 38:49.400
need to unpack that.

38:49.500 --> 38:49.800
Okay, Mon,

38:49.860 --> 38:50.320
what else?

38:50.320 --> 38:51.380
Second, there's

38:51.380 --> 38:52.120
the vector flow.

38:52.840 --> 38:53.680
This represents

38:53.680 --> 38:54.780
the directed motion,

38:55.240 --> 38:56.060
the bulk, flex,

38:56.160 --> 38:56.720
or flow

38:56.720 --> 38:57.680
of the local medium

38:57.680 --> 38:58.500
how energy,

38:58.840 --> 38:59.800
information, momentum

38:59.800 --> 39:00.300
are moving

39:00.300 --> 39:01.440
through a region of space.

39:01.520 --> 39:02.260
So like currents

39:02.260 --> 39:03.000
in the plenum.

39:03.120 --> 39:03.900
Kind of, yeah.

39:04.240 --> 39:04.780
And third,

39:04.940 --> 39:06.320
the entropic modulus, S.

39:06.680 --> 39:07.360
This isn't just

39:07.360 --> 39:08.040
a simple measure

39:08.040 --> 39:08.540
of disorder

39:08.540 --> 39:09.220
like in basic

39:09.220 --> 39:09.920
thermodynamics.

39:10.280 --> 39:11.280
It's more nuanced.

39:12.020 --> 39:13.060
It quantifies disorder,

39:13.200 --> 39:13.860
yes, but also

39:13.860 --> 39:14.740
thermal dispersion.

39:15.080 --> 39:15.760
Rates of change,

39:15.860 --> 39:16.600
directional shifts,

39:16.680 --> 39:17.760
essentially capturing

39:17.760 --> 39:18.880
the full thermodynamic

39:18.880 --> 39:19.840
state and texture

39:19.840 --> 39:21.160
of the local space time.

39:21.160 --> 39:22.180
Hey, PUE best

39:22.180 --> 39:23.080
scalar capacity,

39:23.240 --> 39:23.780
vector flow,

39:23.880 --> 39:24.660
entropic modulus.

39:25.060 --> 39:25.880
And these aren't just

39:25.880 --> 39:27.180
static background values,

39:27.260 --> 39:27.400
right?

39:27.440 --> 39:28.400
They must interact,

39:28.520 --> 39:29.320
evolve dynamically

39:29.320 --> 39:30.620
across all scales.

39:31.080 --> 39:32.240
Oh, absolutely not static.

39:32.360 --> 39:33.460
They're deeply dynamic

39:33.460 --> 39:34.100
and interconnected.

39:34.780 --> 39:35.860
They evolve jointly

39:35.860 --> 39:36.720
under what the framework

39:36.720 --> 39:37.700
calls LAMFRON

39:37.700 --> 39:39.000
and LAMFRIDINE couplings.

39:39.760 --> 39:40.720
LAMFRON describes

39:40.720 --> 39:41.760
the dissipative

39:41.760 --> 39:42.740
processes systems

39:42.740 --> 39:43.360
tending to fall

39:43.360 --> 39:44.540
into lower energy,

39:44.840 --> 39:45.720
higher entropy states

39:45.720 --> 39:46.880
like water flowing

39:46.880 --> 39:47.320
downhill,

39:47.800 --> 39:48.620
things smoothing out.

39:48.620 --> 39:50.080
LAMFRIDINE describes

39:50.080 --> 39:50.680
the opposite,

39:51.160 --> 39:52.220
negentropic recursion,

39:52.460 --> 39:53.280
the emergence of order,

39:53.440 --> 39:54.160
self-organization,

39:54.340 --> 39:55.220
complexity systems,

39:55.600 --> 39:56.740
finding ways to maintain

39:56.740 --> 39:57.620
or even increase

39:57.620 --> 39:58.420
their local structure

39:58.420 --> 39:59.620
by cleverly managing

39:59.620 --> 40:00.420
entropy flows.

40:00.840 --> 40:02.080
These two types of couplings

40:02.080 --> 40:03.460
drive the incredibly rich,

40:03.840 --> 40:04.980
non-linear dynamics

40:04.980 --> 40:05.580
of the system,

40:06.020 --> 40:06.840
allowing RSVP

40:06.840 --> 40:07.720
to potentially account

40:07.720 --> 40:08.460
for complex,

40:08.600 --> 40:09.340
emergent behaviors

40:09.340 --> 40:10.040
at every scale,

40:10.400 --> 40:10.780
from, say,

40:10.860 --> 40:11.660
the quantum foam

40:11.660 --> 40:12.640
to galaxy formation,

40:12.980 --> 40:13.980
maybe even to the evolution

40:13.980 --> 40:14.800
of life itself.

40:15.140 --> 40:15.820
Okay, let's go back

40:15.820 --> 40:16.360
to that phrase

40:16.360 --> 40:17.160
falling outward.

40:17.800 --> 40:18.660
That really jumps out.

40:19.060 --> 40:19.720
It sounds like

40:19.720 --> 40:20.420
more than just

40:20.420 --> 40:21.180
abstract physics.

40:21.360 --> 40:22.100
It feels like it offers

40:22.100 --> 40:23.040
a totally new way

40:23.040 --> 40:24.580
to picture cosmic expansion,

40:25.040 --> 40:26.120
maybe even replacing

40:26.120 --> 40:27.220
the standard accelerating

40:27.220 --> 40:28.480
scale factor idea

40:28.480 --> 40:29.200
with something more,

40:29.420 --> 40:29.940
intrinsic,

40:30.220 --> 40:30.960
self-organizing.

40:31.080 --> 40:31.700
Can you elaborate?

40:32.200 --> 40:33.640
How does space itself

40:33.640 --> 40:34.360
fall outward

40:34.360 --> 40:35.340
or smooth outward?

40:35.700 --> 40:36.680
What does entropic

40:36.680 --> 40:37.860
relaxation dynamics

40:37.860 --> 40:38.800
actually mean

40:38.800 --> 40:39.720
on a cosmic scale?

40:39.720 --> 40:41.040
This is absolutely

40:41.040 --> 40:42.200
a core innovation

40:42.200 --> 40:43.040
in RSVP,

40:43.260 --> 40:44.440
and it directly tackles

40:44.440 --> 40:45.100
the limitations

40:45.100 --> 40:46.280
of that ACDM

40:46.280 --> 40:47.020
encoding ratchet

40:47.020 --> 40:47.600
we discussed.

40:48.360 --> 40:49.100
In RSVP,

40:49.420 --> 40:50.280
space isn't just

40:50.280 --> 40:51.120
an inert stage.

40:51.520 --> 40:52.580
It's a dynamic medium

40:52.580 --> 40:53.320
with its own

40:53.320 --> 40:54.540
thermodynamic properties

40:54.540 --> 40:55.400
governed by that

40:55.400 --> 40:56.480
scalar capacity field

40:56.480 --> 40:57.260
and the entropic

40:57.260 --> 40:57.980
modulus S.

40:58.800 --> 40:59.880
Imagine space itself

40:59.880 --> 41:00.500
having a kind of

41:00.500 --> 41:01.460
entropic potential,

41:01.700 --> 41:02.280
analogous to a

41:02.280 --> 41:03.260
gravitational potential.

41:04.020 --> 41:04.780
Regions with very

41:04.780 --> 41:05.700
low mass density,

41:05.900 --> 41:07.060
the huge cosmic voids

41:07.060 --> 41:08.280
that dominate the universe's

41:08.280 --> 41:08.620
volume,

41:08.620 --> 41:09.720
have a relatively

41:09.720 --> 41:11.060
higher entropic potential.

41:11.380 --> 41:12.300
They are, in a sense,

41:12.380 --> 41:14.040
less relaxed thermodynamically.

41:14.220 --> 41:14.960
To relax,

41:15.300 --> 41:16.560
to minimize this potential,

41:17.080 --> 41:18.080
these voids don't just

41:18.080 --> 41:19.220
passively get stretched

41:19.220 --> 41:20.160
by some external

41:20.160 --> 41:21.160
expansion force.

41:21.700 --> 41:22.100
Instead,

41:22.420 --> 41:23.820
they actively push outward.

41:24.380 --> 41:25.380
They expand intrinsically,

41:25.500 --> 41:26.140
driven by these

41:26.140 --> 41:27.740
entropic relaxation dynamics.

41:27.880 --> 41:28.940
It's like space itself

41:28.940 --> 41:30.240
is trying to smooth out

41:30.240 --> 41:31.520
its own entropic gradients.

41:32.000 --> 41:32.940
Think of it like a pressure,

41:33.260 --> 41:34.100
but it's sourced by

41:34.100 --> 41:34.680
the intrinsic

41:34.680 --> 41:35.820
thermodynamic state

41:35.820 --> 41:36.360
of the plenum,

41:36.760 --> 41:37.900
driven by ANS,

41:37.980 --> 41:38.540
not by some

41:38.540 --> 41:39.060
mysterious,

41:39.580 --> 41:40.500
uniform dark energy

41:40.500 --> 41:41.520
field added on top.

41:41.940 --> 41:42.840
This outward falling

41:42.840 --> 41:43.660
is a dynamic,

41:43.900 --> 41:44.640
spatially varying

41:44.640 --> 41:45.260
field effect,

41:45.800 --> 41:46.560
fundamentally different

41:46.560 --> 41:47.540
from a constant lambda.

41:47.960 --> 41:48.300
So wait,

41:48.720 --> 41:49.560
cosmic expansion,

41:49.700 --> 41:50.540
the stretching of space,

41:50.620 --> 41:51.760
and gravitational contraction,

41:51.900 --> 41:52.580
the pulling together

41:52.580 --> 41:53.020
of matter?

41:53.200 --> 41:53.400
Yeah.

41:53.480 --> 41:54.160
They aren't opposing

41:54.160 --> 41:55.300
forces in RSVP.

41:55.400 --> 41:55.940
They're more like

41:55.940 --> 41:57.460
two sides of the same coin,

41:57.840 --> 41:58.760
a single underlying

41:58.760 --> 41:59.640
entropic principle.

42:00.120 --> 42:00.640
Precisely.

42:00.980 --> 42:02.000
That's the beauty of it.

42:02.420 --> 42:03.520
They are twin directions

42:03.520 --> 42:04.720
of the same fundamental

42:04.720 --> 42:05.620
entropic gradient.

42:06.360 --> 42:07.300
Matter minimizes

42:07.300 --> 42:08.180
its local entropy

42:08.180 --> 42:09.140
by collapsing

42:09.140 --> 42:10.380
inward under gravity,

42:10.380 --> 42:12.180
the familiar mass pole

42:12.180 --> 42:13.000
forming galaxies,

42:13.240 --> 42:13.480
stars,

42:13.640 --> 42:14.020
clusters.

42:14.820 --> 42:15.500
Simultaneously,

42:15.760 --> 42:16.780
the voids minimize

42:16.780 --> 42:17.360
their entropy

42:17.360 --> 42:18.960
by actively expanding

42:18.960 --> 42:19.600
outward through

42:19.600 --> 42:21.120
this intrinsic void push

42:21.120 --> 42:22.520
driven by the plenum's

42:22.520 --> 42:23.160
thermodynamics.

42:23.700 --> 42:25.320
This duality elegantly

42:25.320 --> 42:26.520
explains both the observed

42:26.520 --> 42:27.400
clustering of matter

42:27.400 --> 42:28.480
and the accelerated growth

42:28.480 --> 42:29.120
of voids

42:29.120 --> 42:30.580
without needing to invent

42:30.580 --> 42:32.080
a global accelerating

42:32.080 --> 42:33.020
scale factor

